Paint the **Thunderhead fleet-command HUD kit** — a set of named interface
elements — using only the `paint` and `ui` binaries.

It is the on-screen interface for *Thunderhead*, a naval fleet-command strategy
game: an industrial, riveted-naval overlay with cyan tactical readouts. You are
painting a **kit** of five elements — `command-panel`, `health-bar-frame`,
`minimap-bezel`, `faction-crest`, and `button-primary` — each its own document
of its own declared size.

## How painting works

The only way to make a mark is the two binaries already on your `PATH`:

- **`paint`** — the primary layered raster painter: named layers, alpha
  compositing and blend modes, brushes, gradients, selections, masks, filters,
  and layer effects (bevels, inner shadows, strokes, glows). Use it for the
  painterly work — shading, glows, gradients, grime.
- **`ui`** — the companion crisp UI-composition tool over the same workspace:
  anti-aliased vector shapes, **text** in baked fonts, and **nine-slice**
  authoring. Use it for the structural, pixel-crisp parts — bezels, rails,
  button bodies, labels, and the scalable insets.

They are two front-ends over **one shared workspace and one recorded operation
log**, so you freely interleave them. Both are the sole channel: the emitted
flattened per-element PNGs are the authoritative output, and anything produced
any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded
and flagged — only the recorded operations count.

Every operation targets the element it applies to with `--element <name>`. After
each call the affected element's preview is re-rendered under
`elements/{element}.png` and the operation is appended to the shared
`actions.json`. A pre-seeded `paint.config.json` and a blank workspace per
element are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Run `paint --help` and `ui --help` to list every operation, and `paint
<operation> --help` / `ui <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags —
that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read an element's preview after
your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next; use
`ui nine-slice-preview` to confirm a stretchable element's insets hold before you
finish.

